transcript.text:
The twang followed by a completely free-falling door is very characteristic of a failed door-balance cable/spring, rather than a latch or door issue.

On many Hotpoint LTF integrated models, each hinge is connected to a spring by a cord/cable. Likely scenarios are:

• a cable has snapped or jumped off its pulley;
• a spring has come unhooked or broken;
• less commonly, both sides have failed/disconnected.

Even one side failing can make the door feel dramatically heavier, but no resistance at all suggests checking both sides.

The machine can still latch and wash normally, but don’t let the door drop: it can bend the hinges, strain the decorative cupboard door, or damage the lower edge of the kitchen unit. Support it as you open it.

A repair normally involves pulling the dishwasher out of the cabinet and fitting a compatible door spring / hinge cord kit—often replacing both sides as a pair. The exact LTF suffix matters (for example, LTF 8xxxx, LTF 11xxxx etc.), as spring arrangements and tensions vary with the model and furniture-door weight.

To identify it, look for:
• the full model/industrial code on the sticker around the inner door edge or side;
• occasionally a label on the side of the dishwasher once it is pulled out;
• the appliance paperwork/manual, if available.

I would not try to reconnect the spring with the appliance still installed: access is poor and the springs/cables can be under tension. A domestic appliance engineer should recognise this as a routine door-balance repair.
